// Plain SAYC auctions: 500 deals from mulberry32(31337), dealer board % 4,
// vulnerability by the 16-board cycle. With every convention switched off the
// system must bid exactly these. They are the auctions from before the optional
// conventions existed, except where Stayman or a transfer follows a 2NT rebid
// or opening: opener used to ignore it after 2♣ – 2♦ – 2NT, and responder's
// follow-ups assumed a 20-21 range up to 15 points instead of trying for slam.
//
// Base-judgement fixes then changed 38 of them (board numbers are indexes here):
// - a general invitation now shows a range, so the reply is not a 4NT/6NT leap
//   on any hand: 8, 35, 234, 257, 270, 299, 315, 462, 471, and there is no
//   invitation in a game-forcing 2♣ auction: 345, 421;
// - opener reopens with a double when short in the overcalled suit, and partner
//   passes it with a trump stack: 199, 207, 260, 451;
// - a strong balanced hand (15+) doubles in the balancing seat: 27, 227, 298, 400;
// - after their double, four-card major support goes through Jordan 2NT rather
//   than a redouble (37); opener passes partner's redouble (158); the doubling
//   side does not sit in the redouble (179);
// - 3NT is corrected to four of a major with an eight-card fit and shortness, or
//   a long strong major, before passing: 42, 302, 472; in a game-forcing
//   auction responder shows three-card support for opener's major first (313);
// - slam tries: a raise to four of partner's minor is a forcing slam try (163,
//   246, 390), and slam values ask for aces instead of signing off in game (350);
// - after opener's 2NT rebid, responder plays a six-card major in game (395);
// - raises of 2♣ – 2♦ – 2NT use its 22–24 range, not the 20–21 of a 2NT
//   opening: game with 1–7 (204);
// - each trump beyond eight counts about a point, so long fits compete higher
//   and reach slam more often: 36, 38, 143, 274, 454, 460.
//
// A bidding audit against double-dummy par then changed 49 more (16 better,
// 12 worse, 21 level: +36 IMPs together):
// - a good six-card suit overcalls instead of doubling for takeout: 19, 23,
//   151, 201, 222, 325, 391, 396, 456;
// - a jump raise of partner's minor in competition is preemptive only with
//   five cards (1♣/1♦ may hold three): 10, 42, 89, 114, 165, 169, 176, 285,
//   314, 357, 380, 387;
// - over Michaels the cue bid is in the suit they showed, not in ours, and the
//   double shows values: 57, 184, 274, 498; the takeout doubler accepts
//   partner's jump response with a sound hand: 227;
// - three trumps and 11–12 points without the HCP for a two-over-one make a
//   limit raise, not 1NT: 73, 313;
// - notrump needs the unbid suits stopped (forced notrump too), and in a
//   game-forcing auction a major nobody has bid comes first: 35, 90, 249, 340,
//   345, 386, 472;
// - over a weak two, a misfit hand bids its own good suit and opener raises
//   with three or an honour doubleton: 153, 306, 466, 476; after their overcall
//   of our 1NT a three-level suit is forcing with game values: 154, 196, 477;
//   a new suit at the three level after their overcall: 195, 353; a long suit
//   of one's own is rebid on a misfit: 346;
// - shortness in a suit partner has bid is no longer counted as a ruffing
//   value: 331; and knock-on changes later in the auction: 406, 463, 468.
//
// A second audit against double-dummy par changed 26 more (9 better, 7 worse,
// 10 level: +12 IMPs together):
// - a double of their suit below the three level after we have bid is the
//   competitive double, for takeout, and partner bids (it used to be read as a
//   penalty double, the first double rule): 16, 67, 329, 416, 473, 494; a trump
//   stack no longer doubles there: 369;
// - four trumps and a shapely hand under 13 HCP make a limit raise, not 1NT or
//   a new suit: 295, 302, 331, 392; game values without a fitting notrump or
//   raise bid a forcing new minor over 1♣/1♦ instead of passing: 310, 428;
// - with no room for a cue-bid raise, game values raise partner's major to game
//   in competition: 198, 353, 446; over a doubled weak two, four cards in the
//   other major and 9–11 HCP bid game: 62, 273; after their overcall of our 1NT
//   a long suit counts its length for the forcing three-level bid: 146;
// - a suit slam needs about 32 combined points with a fit (33 in notrump): 203,
//   229, 301, 390; opener shows a second suit with shortness after 2NT: 223,
//   467; and an open-ended invitation no longer draws a quantitative 4NT: 333.
//
// Over their 1NT there is no suit of theirs to cue bid, so the advancer raises
// partner's overcall naturally instead of bidding an artificial 3♣: 134.
//
// Making the bots bid what the lessons teach then changed these:
// - when they bid over partner's single raise, three of opener's suit only
//   competes, by the Law of Total Tricks (no game invitation, no automatic
//   pass): 10, 124, 165, 169, 201;
// - a good raise of partner's overcall starts with the cue bid, and the jump
//   to game is the weak, shapely raise: 59, 276, 387.

